    "A routine is the same agent as Nova, our ticket-to-PR agent, started by an event instead of an assignment. The events are a red build on main, a dependency PR from Renovate that fails CI, a Bug ticket that a team has triaged, and a timer for the weekly cleanup jobs. Everything after the start is shared with Nova: the same harness (our program in the VM that runs the Claude session), the same fresh VM for every run, the same walls and the same review layers. That is the whole design decision of this part of the course. If routines had their own code, we would build and check the safety walls once for Nova and again for every routine, and one of the copies would drift.",
    "So only two things differ, the trigger and the charter. A charter is one short YAML file per routine in the ai-agents repo, under routines/charters/. Three things are involved every time a routine runs: the trigger that starts it, routineRun (the Temporal workflow that every routine runs), and the charter, which routineRun loads first. The charter answers the questions from the story, in a file that is reviewed in a pull request like any code: who owns the routine, what wakes it, where it may write, and what must be true before it may say it is done. Here is the first one, for the frontend CI fixer."
